1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,835
To see how the company came apart from the inside, we
have to go back — to a year before

2
00:00:13,855 --> 00:00:17,138
Hong Kong, and a boardroom on the
twelfth floor.

3
00:00:17,708 --> 00:00:23,342
Catherine Adeyemi worked it out at a board meeting,
in May of twenty thirty-three, by

4
00:00:23,362 --> 00:00:24,739
watching a man smile.

5
00:00:25,307 --> 00:00:31,130
General Thomas Wyatt had joined Umbrella's board when
the Aegis deal closed. He sat across

6
00:00:31,150 --> 00:00:36,907
the table from her, in the boardroom on the twelfth
floor, and he listened to her present

7
00:00:36,927 --> 00:00:42,554
the quarterly accounts, and he smiled at her the
whole time. He smiled at every number.

8
00:00:42,574 --> 00:00:48,072
He smiled at the slide about tax. He smiled until
she looked away, and then, when she

9
00:00:48,092 --> 00:00:50,279
looked back, he was still smiling.

10
00:00:50,848 --> 00:00:56,344
She had known him slightly for a year. She had sat
across from him in the Pentagon, when

11
00:00:56,364 --> 00:01:02,047
he was dying and hiding it and angry at everyone, and
she had liked him — a proud, prickly,

12
00:01:02,067 --> 00:01:07,374
honest old soldier who did not trust anyone in the
room. The man across the table was

13
00:01:07,394 --> 00:01:13,015
twenty pounds lighter, with dark hair, and a grip that
had made her hand ache for an hour.

14
00:01:13,035 --> 00:01:18,467
He agreed with everything the Director said. He had
not asked a single question in four

15
00:01:18,487 --> 00:01:19,032
meetings.

16
00:01:21,102 --> 00:01:26,920
She went to Evelyn. Not the Director. She had
learned, over six years, that the Director

17
00:01:26,940 --> 00:01:32,759
told her one third of the truth, and that Evelyn
Marsh — whatever else she was — did not

18
00:01:32,779 --> 00:01:34,020
lie about the work.

19
00:01:34,709 --> 00:01:36,427
What did you do to Tom Wyatt?

20
00:01:36,947 --> 00:01:37,816
We cured him.

21
00:01:38,335 --> 00:01:39,397
Of what?

22
00:01:39,918 --> 00:01:45,480
Pancreatic cancer. Stage four. He had five months.
He's going to live another forty years,

23
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:48,147
Catherine. Perhaps more. We
don't know yet.

24
00:01:48,667 --> 00:01:49,906
Is he himself?

25
00:01:51,926 --> 00:01:57,261
Evelyn considered the question with genuine interest,
the way she considered everything.

26
00:01:57,950 --> 00:01:59,295
He's better than himself.

27
00:02:01,816 --> 00:02:07,163
Catherine did not say anything else. She thanked
Evelyn, and walked back to the freight

28
00:02:07,183 --> 00:02:12,593
elevator — she had had clearance to the sublevel for
two years now, and had used it four

29
00:02:12,613 --> 00:02:18,022
times — and rode up, and walked out of the old
building, and across the parking lot, and

30
00:02:18,042 --> 00:02:18,947
sat in her car.

31
00:02:19,518 --> 00:02:25,450
Five years before, in a glass office at midnight, she
had asked the Director whether anyone

32
00:02:25,470 --> 00:02:31,076
was being hurt. He had said no. It had been true. He
had made sure it was true, and he

33
00:02:31,096 --> 00:02:34,281
had said it in a way that made it
feel permanent.

34
00:02:34,852 --> 00:02:36,560
It was not true anymore.

35
00:02:38,628 --> 00:02:44,094
She took out her wallet. Behind her own business
card, where it had been for two years

36
00:02:44,114 --> 00:02:49,004
and four months, was a plain white card with a
London telephone number on it.

37
00:02:49,575 --> 00:02:54,994
She looked at it for a long time. Then she drove to
a shopping mall in Christiana, and

38
00:02:55,014 --> 00:02:59,168
bought a phone with cash, and called the
number from the car park.

39
00:02:59,858 --> 00:03:02,448
Miss Adeyemi. How very nice to
hear from you.

40
00:03:02,967 --> 00:03:05,016
You said somebody would always answer.

41
00:03:05,538 --> 00:03:08,129
And somebody has. What would you
like to talk about?

42
00:03:10,150 --> 00:03:11,478
Where the money goes.

43
00:03:14,998 --> 00:03:16,528
He knew within the hour.

44
00:03:17,098 --> 00:03:22,466
Not from the phone — she had been careful, and the
phone was clean. From her face, the

45
00:03:22,486 --> 00:03:28,356
next morning, in the corridor on the eleventh floor,
when she said good morning. The Cold-Read

46
00:03:28,376 --> 00:03:33,869
had been reading Catherine Adeyemi for six years. It
had seen her angry, and afraid, and

47
00:03:33,889 --> 00:03:39,320
exhilarated, and exhausted, and triumphant. It had
never seen what it saw that morning,

48
00:03:39,340 --> 00:03:43,266
and it took him a moment, standing in
the corridor, to name it.

49
00:03:43,836 --> 00:03:49,875
It was relief. The particular, bone-deep relief of a
person who has been carrying something

50
00:03:49,895 --> 00:03:54,269
heavy for a very long time, and has
finally put it down somewhere.

51
00:03:56,460 --> 00:03:59,299
She has betrayed you. To the English.

52
00:03:59,818 --> 00:04:00,458
Yes.

53
00:04:00,979 --> 00:04:06,986
I told you. Five years ago. A woman who asks where
the money goes will one day ask where

54
00:04:07,006 --> 00:04:11,370
the bodies are. You should have replaced
her with a second Pell.

55
00:04:11,889 --> 00:04:14,152
And then who would have told me
when I was wrong?

56
00:04:14,673 --> 00:04:19,239
You do not need to be told when you are
wrong. You need to be obeyed.

57
00:04:21,259 --> 00:04:27,079
Wesker did not answer that immediately. He stood at
the window of the corner office, on

58
00:04:27,099 --> 00:04:32,786
the twelfth floor of the tower, looking across the
river at the old building, where a

59
00:04:32,806 --> 00:04:37,687
light was on in a third-floor window that
nobody used anymore except him.

60
00:04:38,377 --> 00:04:44,009
Everyone betrays, Adolf. Everyone. Your generals.
Your party. Your own doctors, in the

61
00:04:44,029 --> 00:04:49,728
end. Everyone who is not owned betrays, sooner or
later, because everyone has something

62
00:04:49,748 --> 00:04:51,306
they love more than you.

63
00:04:51,706 --> 00:04:57,018
The trick is not to prevent it. The trick is to own
the route their betrayal travels.

64
00:04:58,538 --> 00:05:01,035
You will let her keep talking to them.

65
00:05:01,555 --> 00:05:07,058
I'll let her tell Julian Ashcombe everything she
knows. Which is, as it happens, a very

66
00:05:07,078 --> 00:05:12,646
great deal about money, and almost nothing about
anything else. And Julian Ashcombe will

67
00:05:12,666 --> 00:05:18,043
build his whole picture of us out of the money. And
the money, Adolf, is the least of

68
00:05:18,063 --> 00:05:18,805
what we are.

69
00:05:21,324 --> 00:05:27,115
That was the reason he gave. It was a good reason.
It was, as far as it went, true.

70
00:05:27,685 --> 00:05:33,493
But the Cold-Read does not work on its owner. And
so Albert Wesker never read his own

71
00:05:33,513 --> 00:05:39,526
face that morning, at the window, and never saw what
the old voice saw, from inside, and

72
00:05:39,546 --> 00:05:41,856
chose — for once — not to mention.

73
00:05:42,427 --> 00:05:44,211
He did not want to lose her.

74
00:05:46,779 --> 00:05:52,933
In the spring of twenty thirty-four, Umbrella Life
Sciences became the most valuable company

75
00:05:52,953 --> 00:05:54,812
in the history of the world.

76
00:05:55,382 --> 00:06:01,777
Two and a half trillion dollars. Four hundred and
twenty thousand employees. Aeterna in

77
00:06:01,797 --> 00:06:08,487
the medicine cabinets of sixty million people. The
assay in every hospital in the developed

78
00:06:08,507 --> 00:06:15,271
world. Halvorsen Pike's century of medicines. Aegis
Dynamics' drones. Lumen's drug-discovery

79
00:06:15,291 --> 00:06:21,760
servers. A research partnership with China. A
classified contract with the Pentagon. And

80
00:06:21,780 --> 00:06:28,470
a foundation that ran clinics in forty-one countries,
and was — by some measures — a larger

81
00:06:28,490 --> 00:06:33,927
provider of rural healthcare in Africa than
the World Health Organization.

82
00:06:34,497 --> 00:06:36,363
It had begun to build towns.

83
00:06:36,932 --> 00:06:43,130
Not metaphorically. Actual towns. In Wilmington,
where the old industrial park had become

84
00:06:43,150 --> 00:06:49,278
a campus, and the streets around the campus had
become Umbrella housing, and the schools

85
00:06:49,298 --> 00:06:55,356
had become Umbrella schools. In Singapore, beside
the plant. In Basel, on land that had

86
00:06:55,376 --> 00:07:01,364
once belonged to Halvorsen Pike. In each of them,
the houses were good, and cheap, and

87
00:07:01,384 --> 00:07:07,441
clean. The clinics were free. The schools were
excellent. And in each of them, at eight

88
00:07:07,461 --> 00:07:13,449
o'clock every morning, children who had never set
foot in a laboratory put their right

89
00:07:13,469 --> 00:07:18,340
hands on their chests, in their classrooms,
and said eighty-one words.

90
00:07:18,910 --> 00:07:25,117
Nobody had told the schools to do that. The teachers
had simply started, one by one, because

91
00:07:25,137 --> 00:07:28,435
their parents did it at work, and
it seemed nice.

92
00:07:31,254 --> 00:07:34,459
Assessment. Children. Belief, forming.

93
00:07:38,935 --> 00:07:40,677
Children. Belief, forming.

94
00:07:41,247 --> 00:07:46,990
The Wesker half of the mind read that line, and felt
— for the first time in eight years

95
00:07:47,009 --> 00:07:52,686
— something close to distaste. It was not a moral
feeling. It was aesthetic. He did not

96
00:07:52,706 --> 00:07:58,514
like mess, and children were messy, and belief in
children was the messiest thing of all.

97
00:07:59,084 --> 00:08:04,637
The Adolf half read it, and remembered other
classrooms, in another country, in another

98
00:08:04,657 --> 00:08:09,314
century. And it said nothing, because there
was nothing it needed to say.

99
00:08:11,884 --> 00:08:15,833
Walter Pruitt died in October, in his
sleep, at seventy-two.

100
00:08:16,403 --> 00:08:22,624
He had retired in the autumn of twenty twenty-nine, and
moved into a small house in Elsmere,

101
00:08:22,644 --> 00:08:28,390
and spent five years fishing on the Brandywine and
doing crosswords and not sleeping,

102
00:08:28,410 --> 00:08:34,360
as he always had. His son came from Colorado for the
funeral, and his granddaughter, and

103
00:08:34,380 --> 00:08:38,973
a few men from the Legion hall, and Dana
Whitfield, and Tomás Reyes.

104
00:08:39,543 --> 00:08:45,150
And, at the back of the small brick church, standing
alone in a black coat with his hands

105
00:08:45,170 --> 00:08:47,490
folded in front of him, the Director.

106
00:08:48,059 --> 00:08:53,847
He had not been asked. Nobody had known he would come.
He stood through the whole service,

107
00:08:53,867 --> 00:08:59,460
and did not sit down, and did not speak to anyone,
and when it was over he went outside

108
00:08:59,480 --> 00:09:03,783
and stood by the church door while the
coffin was carried past him.

109
00:09:04,354 --> 00:09:06,905
He took off his sunglasses as it went by.

110
00:09:09,095 --> 00:09:10,718
Why are we here?

111
00:09:11,236 --> 00:09:16,582
He was the first one. The first person in this world
who looked at me and knew. The first

112
00:09:16,602 --> 00:09:19,537
night. In the washroom doorway.
Different animal.

113
00:09:20,057 --> 00:09:21,423
He was a guard.

114
00:09:21,943 --> 00:09:25,699
He was a witness. I'd like to be here for
the end of the first one.

115
00:09:27,719 --> 00:09:33,722
The old voice did not argue. It had watched, from
the inside, a great many funerals in

116
00:09:33,742 --> 00:09:40,096
its first life — state funerals, with drums and
torches and flags, for men it had sometimes

117
00:09:40,116 --> 00:09:46,120
had killed. It had never stood at the back of a
small church for an old soldier it had

118
00:09:46,140 --> 00:09:47,872
not needed anything from.

119
00:09:48,442 --> 00:09:52,072
It found, to its own surprise, that
it did not mind.

120
00:09:54,640 --> 00:10:00,625
Walter's son gave Dana a box, afterwards. A few
things from his father's house that he

121
00:10:00,645 --> 00:10:06,559
thought she might want. Walter's old security badge
from Division Seven. A photograph

122
00:10:06,579 --> 00:10:12,494
of the staff from twenty twenty-five, the year
before everything, in front of the old

123
00:10:12,514 --> 00:10:14,588
faded blue sign. A coffee mug.

124
00:10:15,158 --> 00:10:21,781
And a small spiral notebook, very worn, the kind that
fits in a breast pocket, with nearly

125
00:10:21,801 --> 00:10:25,176
every page filled in Walter's
blocky capitals.

126
00:10:25,745 --> 00:10:30,014
Dana read it at her kitchen table that
night, from beginning to end.

127
00:10:30,585 --> 00:10:37,246
Camera doesn't see him. Nobody home but the orders. Gus
says the unit was here since oh-nine.

128
00:10:37,266 --> 00:10:43,353
It wasn't. Lights in the basement never go off.
Evelyn went down at nine, not up till

129
00:10:43,373 --> 00:10:49,820
six. New guard doesn't blink. None of the new ones
blink. They're saying it like soldiers.

130
00:10:50,389 --> 00:10:56,635
And on the first page, inside the cover, in the same
blocky capitals, dated the week the

131
00:10:56,655 --> 00:10:58,059
Silent Ones arrived:

132
00:10:58,629 --> 00:11:04,153
If something happens to me, give this to the
reporter. Price. She'll know what to do with

133
00:11:04,173 --> 00:11:04,340
it.

134
00:11:06,909 --> 00:11:12,269
Dana Whitfield sat at her kitchen table until three
in the morning, holding the notebook.

135
00:11:12,839 --> 00:11:18,337
She had worked for the Director for eight years. He
had saved her job, and her colleagues'

136
00:11:18,357 --> 00:11:23,733
jobs, and paid for her sister's hip replacement, and
made her chief of staff of the most

137
00:11:23,753 --> 00:11:29,129
valuable company on Earth, and she was grateful to
him in a way that frightened her, and

138
00:11:29,149 --> 00:11:33,974
she was frightened of him in a way that she had
stopped letting herself notice.

139
00:11:34,543 --> 00:11:40,044
She read the first page again. If something happens
to me, give this to the reporter.

140
00:11:40,615 --> 00:11:46,782
Walter had never asked anyone for anything. Not once,
in all the years she had known him.

141
00:11:49,351 --> 00:11:55,331
She posted it in the morning, from a post office in
Newark, Delaware, where nobody knew

142
00:11:55,351 --> 00:11:58,710
her, in a padded envelope with no
return address.

143
00:11:59,280 --> 00:12:05,281
Of all the betrayals the Director would suffer in his
long life — and there would be many,

144
00:12:05,301 --> 00:12:11,301
and some would be enormous — it was the smallest. A
notebook, in an envelope, from a woman

145
00:12:11,321 --> 00:12:17,054
who loved him out of gratitude and fear and habit,
to a woman who wanted to understand

146
00:12:17,074 --> 00:12:17,322
him.

147
00:12:17,890 --> 00:12:21,658
He never found out. It was the only
one he never found out.

148
00:12:22,229 --> 00:12:28,295
And it ended up — exactly as the narrator promised
you, a very long time ago — in the

149
00:12:28,315 --> 00:12:30,730
hands of exactly the wrong person.
